[[:db/cas 17592186314747 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Robin boundary conditions, given by\n\n$$\ny(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, setting ${\\alpha }_{1}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{1}=1,$ ${\\alpha }_{2}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{2}=-1,$ and $a=0,$  $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ becomes\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0)+{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}(0)+{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-3m+2 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have ${m}_{1}=1$ and ${m}_{2}=2,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0}+{e}^{0} & {e}^{3(0)}+2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{1}-{e}^{1} & {e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{2(1)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =2(-{e}^{2})-0(3) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has a unique solution }y≡0.$" "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Robin boundary conditions, given by\n\n$$\ny(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, setting ${\\alpha }_{1}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{1}=1,$ ${\\alpha }_{2}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{2}=-1,$ and $a=0,$  $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ becomes\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}(0)+{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}(0)+{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-3m+2 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have ${m}_{1}=1$ and ${m}_{2}=2,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{e}^{0} & {e}^{3(0)}+2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{1}-{e}^{1} & {e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{2(1)}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =2(-{e}^{2})-0(3) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has a unique solution }y≡0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314752 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186314752 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we solve the auxiliary equation\n\n$$\n{m}^{2}+1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich has roots $\\pm i.$ Thus, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0(-1)-1(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions.}$" "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we solve the auxiliary equation\n\n$$\n{m}^{2}+1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich has roots $\\pm i.$ Thus, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0(-1)-1(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions.}$"] [:db/cas 17592186314753 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-1,1⟩,\\,p=⟨2,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x-y-2z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-1,1⟩,\\,p=⟨2,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x-y-2z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314772 :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 5t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314772 :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (5t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(2{t}^{2})}^{′} \\\\ {(2t-8)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{2t})}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{3}(t)$ only\"." "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (5t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 5t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 5t\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{(2{t}^{2})}^{′} \\\\ {(2t-8)}^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{({e}^{2t})}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{t})}^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{3}(t)$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186314773 :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314773 :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{-t})}^{′} \\\\ {(0)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(0)}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{2t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(t)}^{′} \\\\ {(t+1)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only\"." "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{({e}^{-t})}^{′} \\\\ {(0)}^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{(0)}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{2t})}^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{(t)}^{′} \\\\ {(t+1)}^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186314774 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\