rix}x(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186314813 :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n- **I.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n- **I.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186314813 :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186314840 :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314840 :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t{e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (t{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"." "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t{e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (t{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186314841 :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314841 :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"." "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & \\ne \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186314967 :question/problem "Find $k$, if $2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find $k$, if $2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314967 :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+k[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $k=-3.$" "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $k=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314969 :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a-5x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a-5x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314969 :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-5x & =b \\\\ -5x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}([\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3-1=-4.$" "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-5x & =b \\\\ -5x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3-1=-4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186314972 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314972 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315058 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186315058 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 31\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=31,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $b$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 31\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=31,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $b$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315059 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186315059 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 18 & -21 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-18{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+6(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-3){a}_{2}+(-1){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot (-1)=6.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 18 & -21 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-18{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+6(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-3){a}_{2}+(-1){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot (-1)=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186315094 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]?$" "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186315094 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}=-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186315124 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186315186 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186315200 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186315249 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186315268 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" "\\beg