5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 17 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186316109 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186316304 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186316517 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186317166 :question/problem "If $p=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, then $p\\cdot q=$" "If $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$, then $p\\cdot q=$"] [:db/cas 17592186317169 :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{4} \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{-2} \\\\ -8x \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ If $u\\cdot v=12$ and $x<6$, find the value of $x.$" "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{4} \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{-2} \\\\ -8x \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $u\\cdot v=12$ and $x<6$, find the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317172 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186317208 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t=6 \\\\ 7s+10t=9 \\\\ -3s-4t=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{66}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+28{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t=6 \\\\ t=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-8t & =6 \\\\ s-8(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =6 \\\\ s+4 & =6 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b-\\frac{1}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.$" "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t=6 \\\\ 7s+10t=9 \\\\ -3s-4t=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{66}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+28{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t=6 \\\\ t=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-8t & =6 \\\\ s-8(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =6 \\\\ s+4 & =6 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b-\\frac{1}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317213 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317213 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=-14 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 6 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+(-2){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-16.$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=-14 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 6 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+(-2){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-16.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317226 :question/problem "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].$ Find $r+s.$" "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $s=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $r+s.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317226 :question/worked-solution "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+1 \\\\ 2+5 \\\\ -3+2 \\\\ 1-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+1 \\\\ 2+5 \\\\ -3+2 \\\\ 1-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317257 :question/problem "Find $j$, if ${a}_{1}-j{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find $j$, if ${a}_{1}-j{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317257 :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $j$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-j{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-j[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ j[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ j[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $j=2.$" "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $j$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-j{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $j=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317259 :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186317259 :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation is a false statement. Hence,the system has no solution." "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation is a false statement. Hence,the system has no solution."] [:db/cas 17592186317317 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]?$" "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186317317 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186317320 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186317320 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true." "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186317349 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186317387 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317387 :question/worked-solution "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k+9 \\\\ 2k+18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k+9=0 \\\\ 2k+18=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-9.$" "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}k+9 \\\\ 2k+18\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k+9=0 \\\\ 2k+18=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317403 :question/problem "The matrix $D$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(D).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}+{x}_{4}{d}_{4},$ and ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, ${d}_{3}$, and ${d}_{4}$ are respective columns of $D.$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $D$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(D).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}+{x}_{4}{d}_{4},$ and ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, ${d}_{3}$, and ${d}_{4}$ are respective columns of $D.$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317403 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{4}=11 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=-2 \\\\ 0=9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get a false statement $0=9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{4}=11 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=-2 \\\\ 0=9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get a false statement $0=9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317416 :question/problem "The matrix $E$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{e}_{1}+{x}_{2}{e}_{2}+{x}_{3}{e}_{3}+{x}_{4}{e}_{4},$ and ${e}_{1}$, ${e}_{2}$, ${e}_{3}$, and ${e}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $E$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{e}_{1}+{x}_{2}{e}_{2}+{x}_{3}{e}_{3}+{x}_{4}{e}_{4},$ and ${e}_{1}$, ${e}_{2}$, ${e}_{3}$, and ${e}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317416 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=4 \\\\ 0=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=4 \\\\ 0=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317417 :question/problem "The matrix $F$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2},$ and ${f}_{1},$  ${f}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $F.$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $F$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2},$ and ${f}_{1},$  ${f}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $F.$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317417 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(F),$ we need to check if the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[F\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[F\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=1.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=1\\cdot (-2)=-2.$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(F),$ we need to check if the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[F\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[F\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=1.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=1\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=1\\cdot (-2)=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317441 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317441 :question/worked-solution "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 90\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-59 \\\\ 118 \\\\ -33\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 180\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vector lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$" "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 90\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-59 \\\\ 118 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 180\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vector lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317454 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317454 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$" "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317510 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317510 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\